@meta
id: 26-09
title: Line the columns up
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: an alignment extension, or the space bar, many times
open: orders.fixture.txt

@brief
A fixture table that someone appended two rows to without lining anything
up. Every value is separated by at least one space; they should be
separated by enough spaces that the columns agree.

`column -t` reads whitespace-separated fields and prints them padded to
the widest value in each column. It is on every machine you will ever ssh
into and almost nobody knows it is there.

The five table lines are lines 2 to 6. Select them and filter the
selection: the comment on line 1 is prose and would be mangled.

@keys
| `V` then a motion | select whole lines |
| `:` in visual mode | starts a command line already reading `:'<,'>` |
| `:'<,'>!column -t` | the selected lines through `column -t` |

@hints
Make a linewise visual selection over the five table rows first.
--
With a selection live, pressing `:` fills in the range for you — you do
not type `'<,'>`, it is already there. Then `!column -t`.
--
`j`, `V4j`, `:!column -t` Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    j  V4j  :!column -t<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes to align a table that would take a minute and a half
with the space bar, and the result is right rather than nearly right.

The visual-then-colon habit is the one to take away. Any time the block
you want is easier to *see* than to address — a table, a selection you
made with `V}`, a ragged log extract — select it and press `:`. Vim
writes `'<,'>` for you and every ex command you know works on exactly
what is highlighted: `:'<,'>sort`, `:'<,'>s/x/y/g`, `:'<,'>!column -t`,
`:'<,'>!sort -u`.

Two notes on `column -t` itself, since you will reach for it again.
Fields are split on runs of whitespace, so it cannot align anything where
a value contains a space. And `-s,` changes the separator: `:%!column -s,
-t` turns a CSV into something readable, which is the fastest way to make
sense of a dumped file. Turning it back is `:%!column -t -o,` or, more
often, `u`.

@start orders.fixture.txt
# seed rows for the order fixtures, keep the columns lined up
id status total currency
ord_1001 SHIPPED 42.50 GBP
ord_1002 PENDING 7.00 GBP
ord_1003 CANCELLED 129.99 EUR
ord_1004 REFUNDED 8.25 USD

@expected orders.fixture.txt
# seed rows for the order fixtures, keep the columns lined up
id        status     total   currency
ord_1001  SHIPPED    42.50   GBP
ord_1002  PENDING    7.00    GBP
ord_1003  CANCELLED  129.99  EUR
ord_1004  REFUNDED   8.25    USD
